Both are good examples of the same underlying rule. Here's what the code actually shows.

## Why breadcrumb UI is in `layout`, not `elements`/`components`

Look at what the breadcrumb components import:

`layout/navigation/components/breadcrumb/Breadcrumb.svelte`:
```ts
import { navigation } from "@21n/layout/navigation/navigation";
import { Resource } from "@nucleum/datafn/resource.enum";
// ...
navigation.gotoResource(item.resourceId.split(":")[0] as Resource, item.resourceId);
```

`layout/navigation/components/breadcrumbs/Breadcrumbs.svelte`:
```ts
import { navigation } from "@21n/layout/navigation/navigation";
// ...
if (item.path) navigation.gotoPath(item.path);
else navigation.resourceClickHandler(...);
```

That settles it mechanically. `elements` and `components` **cannot import (even transitively) navigation, DataFn, or products** — `check:architecture` rejects it. The breadcrumb imports `navigation` (layout-owned URL/history routing) and the DataFn `Resource` enum. So it is structurally disqualified from the portable layers.

But there's a conceptual reason too, not just the lint rule. **A breadcrumb *is* navigation.** It represents your position in the app's location hierarchy and clicking it routes you there. That's precisely what `client/layout` owns: "how the app is arranged and moved through." The docs put URL/history and resource navigation in `layout/navigation/navigation.ts`, and the breadcrumb sits right next to it in `layout/navigation/components/`.

Note the split within the breadcrumb itself, which shows the boundary in action:
- `BreadcrumbItemView.svelte` is the dumb visual chip (label, truncation, hover). That's the portable-style part.
- `Breadcrumb.svelte` / `Breadcrumbs.svelte` compose those chips **and wire them to `navigation`**. That composition is the layout concern.

If someone wanted a truly reusable breadcrumb, the pattern would be: keep the visual chip primitive in `elements`, and pass navigation in as a prop or host contract from the owner. They chose not to abstract it because breadcrumbs here are inherently a navigation surface, so the whole thing lives with navigation in `layout`.

## Why `account-session.ts` and `account.store.ts` are split

They live in different layers because they do fundamentally different jobs, and each is allowed different imports.

**`runtime/account/account.store.ts` — pure reactive state.** The entire file:
```ts
const state = writable<UserAccount>();
const account = { subscribe, set, update, get };
export default account;
```
No navigation, no persistence, no workflow. Just "what is the current account session, reactively." Runtime is the bottom layer and cannot import UI, features, or application composition, so this file has essentially zero dependencies. Anything at any layer can subscribe to it safely.

**`application/account/account-session.ts` — the workflow that drives that state.** Look at its imports:
```ts
import { navigation } from "@21n/layout/navigation/navigation";
import { Persistence } from "@nucleum/persistence/persistence";
import { destroyNucleumDatafn } from "@nucleum/datafn/datafn.store";
import { postDataToParent } from "@nucleum/client/runtime/embed/embed.utils";
import account from "@nucleum/client/runtime/account/account.store";
```
It orchestrates `init`, `signIn`, `signOut`, `bootstrap`, redirects after auth (`navigation.gotoPath`), tears down DataFn on sign-out, writes to `clientStorage`, and posts to the native embed. It *reads and writes* `account.store` but adds all the cross-cutting lifecycle around it.

The reason they can't be one file: that workflow imports layout (`navigation`), persistence, and DataFn. If you put it in `runtime/account/account.store.ts`, runtime would be importing layout and DataFn, which the architecture forbids and which would create cycles (navigation and DataFn ultimately depend on account state). Keeping the bare `writable` in runtime lets the low layers and portable UI observe session state without dragging the entire sign-in machinery down with them.

The pattern generalizes: **state that everything needs to observe sits low (runtime); the workflow that mutates it during real user flows sits high (application), where importing everything is allowed.** The docs state this exact split: "Reactive account session state lives with the account runtime... Authentication persistence, bootstrap, sign-in, sign-out, navigation, and DataFn teardown are coordinated by `client/application/account/account-session.ts`."

